@startuml
title ComponentStorage<T> — How the ECS Storage Works

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam defaultTextAlignment left

class "ComponentStorage<T>" as Storage {
  - components_ : std::vector<T>
  - entities_ : std::vector<Entity>
  - entityToIndex_ : std::unordered_map<Entity, std::size_t>
  --
  + add(entity, component)
  + has(entity) : bool
  + get(entity) : T&
  + get(entity) const : const T&
  + components() : std::vector<T>&
  + entities() const : const std::vector<Entity>&
  + remove(entity)
}

note top of Storage
ComponentStorage<T> keeps three data structures synchronized:

1. components_(i) = the component data
2. entities_(i) = the Entity that owns that component
3. entityToIndex_(entity) = i

The shared index i connects them.
end note

package "Example: ComponentStorage<Velocity>" {

  object "components_" as Components {
    index 0 : Velocity A
    index 1 : Velocity B
    index 2 : Velocity C
    index 3 : Velocity D
  }

  object "entities_" as Entities {
    index 0 : Entity 10
    index 1 : Entity 42
    index 2 : Entity 71
    index 3 : Entity 99
  }

  object "entityToIndex_" as Lookup {
    Entity 10 : index 0
    Entity 42 : index 1
    Entity 71 : index 2
    Entity 99 : index 3
  }
}

Storage --> Components : owns
Storage --> Entities : owns
Storage --> Lookup : owns

Lookup ..> Entities : index
Lookup ..> Components : same index

note right of Lookup
Example lookup:

get(Entity 42)

Entity 42
  -> index 1
  -> components_(1)
  -> Velocity B
end note

@enduml